  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "For honest concurrent use, section 44(3)(a) gives the Registrar a discretion to accept an application, subject to conditions or limitations, if satisfied that there has been honest concurrent use of the two trade marks.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(3) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied: (a) that there has been honest concurrent use of the 2 trade marks; or (b) that, because of other circumstances, it is proper to do so; the Registrar may accept the application for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark subject to any conditions or limitations that the Registrar thinks fit to impose."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s practice, explain why you adopted your trade mark, when you knew of the earlier trade mark between first use and filing, and why you nevertheless chose to use yours.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/6~2",
          "quote": "The applicant should explain why they adopted the trade mark and whether they were aware of the earlier trade mark at any point from their first use up to the filing date of the application."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/6~2",
          "quote": "If they were aware of the prior mark, they should indicate why they nevertheless chose to use their mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Explain what enquiries you made before commencing use, or why you made none; a bare assertion of honest use is insufficient under the Manual.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4",
          "quote": "Examiners should not accept bare statements that a trade mark was used honestly or that an applicant was not aware of the earlier trade mark without any explanation as to what enquiries were made prior to commencing use, or the reasons that no enquiries were made."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Knowing of the earlier trade mark does not by itself rule out honesty, but the Manual says it will ordinarily weigh strongly against honesty and you must establish why your use was nevertheless honest.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4",
          "quote": "Mere knowledge of the prior trade mark(s) does not (without more) preclude a finding that an applicant has acted honestly (Zip Co, [62], citing McCormick & Company Inc v McCormick [2000] FCA 1335, [33]), but it will ordinarily weigh strongly against a finding of honesty (Zip Co at [70]). Where the applicant was aware of the prior trade mark(s), it will be necessary for the applicant to establish why their use of the trade mark was nevertheless honest."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If your explanation is that you believed consumers would not be confused and you did not intend to trade off the earlier mark’s goodwill, give cogent reasons for that belief and supporting evidence where available.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4",
          "quote": "For example, this could be because the applicant considered that consumers would not experience confusion and had no intention to trade off the goodwill of the prior registered mark. The applicant’s case will generally be more persuasive if they provide cogent reasons for forming this belief, and/or supporting evidence."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The examiner may require a detailed explanation of how your belief about confusion was formed, including the information available to you and the searches you say you undertook.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4",
          "quote": "Examiners should also carefully consider statements concerning an applicant’s belief that the use of their trade mark would not be likely to cause confusion. In some cases, it may be appropriate to require a detailed explanation of the basis for that belief. This may assist in assessing the applicant’s subjective state of mind, including how that belief was formed having regard to the circumstances of the particular case, such as the information available to the applicant and the searches it claims to have undertaken."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If you made no enquiries, explain why your use was nevertheless honest, recognising that the Manual treats deliberate avoidance of searches or deliberate disregard of results as making honesty harder to establish.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~3",
          "quote": "Where an applicant has failed to make enquiries, they should be able to explain why, despite this, their use of the trade mark was honest."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~3",
          "quote": "Recklessness or wilful blindness (either avoiding searching or deliberately ignoring certain results) will make it difficult to discharge the onus to establish honesty."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Your explanation should directly address your actual state of mind in its surrounding circumstances, because the Manual places the onus of proving honest use on you.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part28/3/3/1~1",
          "quote": "Whether the use was honest is based on the applicant’s subjective state of mind assessed against the standards of ordinary, decent people (Zip Co, [72]). The applicant’s state of mind should be addressed directly by the applicant, informed by the surrounding facts and circumstances. The applicant, seeking to establish honest concurrent use, bears the onus of proof and must establish that their use was honest."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
